# Code Review and Merge Workflow

## Goal

Define the end-to-end lifecycle from writeup through implementation to code review and merge, including all the git-meta keys needed to store review state. This closes the loop on ticgit's workflow: ideas become tickets, tickets become branches, branches get reviewed, and reviews get merged.

## The Full Lifecycle

```
writeup → ticket → sub-issues + specs → implement → code link → review → approve → merge → close
```

### Phase 1: Writeup (already implemented)

An idea starts as a writeup with versioned content.

```
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:title           (string)
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:status          (string)  open | closed
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:versions        (list)    versioned markdown content
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:authors         (set)     contributor emails
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:tags            (set)
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:tickets         (set)     linked ticket UUIDs
```

Commands: `ti writeup new`, `ti writeup edit`, `ti writeup link`

### Phase 2: Ticket + Sub-issues + Spec (already implemented)

Promote a writeup to a ticket, break it down, add implementation specs.

```
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:title            (string)
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:spec             (string)  implementation specification
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:parent           (string)  parent ticket UUID
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:children         (set)     child ticket UUIDs
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:writeups         (set)     linked writeup UUIDs
```

Commands: `ti writeup promote`, `ti new --subissue`, `ti spec`

### Phase 3: Implementation + Code Link (partially implemented)

Developer works on a branch, links it to the ticket.

```
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:code             (string)  https://host/repo:branch
```

Currently `code` is a single string field. This works for simple cases but doesn't track the full picture. See "What's Missing" below.

### Phase 4: Review (NEW - needs design)

This is the gap. Once code is written, we need a way to:
1. Request a review
2. Track who's reviewing
3. Leave review comments (distinct from ticket comments)
4. Approve or request changes
5. Sign off on the final state
6. Merge

## What's Missing: Review Data Model

### Option A: Reviews as a sub-namespace under tickets

Store review state directly on the ticket:

```
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:branch      (string)  branch name
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:base         (string)  base branch (e.g. main)
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:head         (string)  head commit SHA
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:status       (string)  open | approved | changes-requested | merged | closed
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:reviewers    (set)     requested reviewer emails
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:approvals    (set)     emails who approved
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:comments     (list)    review-specific comments
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:created-at   (string)  RFC3339
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:created-by   (string)  email
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:merged-at    (string)  RFC3339 (set on merge)
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews:<review-uuid>:merged-by    (string)  email
```

**Pros:** Everything lives under the ticket. Simple queries.
**Cons:** Deep nesting. A review is conceptually its own entity.

### Option B: Reviews as a top-level namespace (recommended)

```
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:title            (string)  defaults to ticket title
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:ticket           (string)  linked ticket UUID
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:branch           (string)  branch name
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:base             (string)  base branch
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:head             (string)  head commit SHA at time of review request
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:status           (string)  open | approved | changes-requested | merged | closed
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:reviewers        (set)     requested reviewer emails
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:approvals        (set)     emails who approved
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:comments         (list)    review comments (JSON: author, body, commit, path, line)
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:created-at       (string)  RFC3339
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:created-by       (string)  email
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:merged-at        (string)  RFC3339
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:merged-by        (string)  email
```

Plus a backlink on the ticket:
```
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews          (set)     review UUIDs linked to this ticket
```

**Pros:** Follows the same pattern as writeups (own namespace, bidirectional links). Reviews are first-class. Cleaner key paths.
**Cons:** Slightly more work to implement bidirectional links (but we already do this for writeups).

### Option C: Use git-meta branch targets instead of project targets

Since reviews are inherently about branches, we could attach review metadata to the branch itself:

```
target: branch/<branch-name>
key: ticgit:review:status              (string)
key: ticgit:review:ticket              (string)
key: ticgit:review:reviewers           (set)
key: ticgit:review:approvals           (set)
key: ticgit:review:comments            (list)
...
```

**Pros:** Semantically correct - the review IS about the branch. No UUID needed, branch name is the identifier. Natural fit for git-meta's target model.
**Cons:** All other ticgit data uses `project` target. Mixing target types adds complexity. Branch names can be reused. Would need to rethink how `ti sync` works for branch targets.

## Recommendation: Option B (top-level namespace)

This is consistent with how writeups work. Reviews are first-class objects with their own UUIDs, bidirectional links to tickets, and a flat key structure.

## Proposed Commands

```bash
# Create a review from a ticket (uses ticket's code branch or current branch)
ti review new <ticket-id>
ti review new <ticket-id> --branch feature-branch --base main

# List reviews
ti review list
ti review list --status open

# Show review details
ti review show <review-id>

# Add reviewers
ti review add-reviewer <review-id> alice@example.com

# Leave a review comment
ti review comment <review-id> "looks good but check the edge case on line 42"

# Approve
ti review approve <review-id>

# Request changes
ti review request-changes <review-id> "needs error handling in the parser"

# Update head commit (after pushing new changes)
ti review update <review-id>
ti review update <review-id> --head abc123

# Merge (records the merge, doesn't do git merge)
ti review merge <review-id>

# Or actually do the merge
ti review merge <review-id> --execute
```

## Review Comments Format

Review comments need more structure than ticket comments. Stored as JSON in the list:

```json
{
  "author": "alice@example.com",
  "body": "This function needs bounds checking",
  "type": "comment",
  "commit": "abc123",
  "path": "src/parser.rs",
  "line": 42
}
```

Types: `comment`, `approval`, `changes-requested`, `resolved`

The `commit`, `path`, and `line` fields are optional - general review comments omit them, inline comments include them.

## Patch Queue Tracking

When a review gets new commits pushed after review comments, we want to track what changed:

```
ticgit:reviews:<uuid>:revisions        (list)    commit SHAs marking review rounds
```

Each entry in revisions is a JSON object:
```json
{
  "head": "abc123",
  "message": "addressed review feedback"
}
```

This lets reviewers see "what changed since I last looked" by diffing between revision entries.

## Full Lifecycle Example

```bash
# 1. Start with a writeup
ti writeup new --title "Add caching layer"
ti writeup edit 075

# 2. Promote to ticket
ti writeup promote 075 --tags feature

# 3. Break into sub-issues
ti new --title "Cache invalidation strategy" --subissue a3f
ti new --title "Redis adapter" --subissue a3f
ti new --title "Cache hit metrics" --subissue a3f

# 4. Add spec to parent ticket
ti spec a3f    # opens editor

# 5. Implement on a branch, link code
ti checkout a3f
ti state in-progress
ti code a3f https://github.com/org/repo:sc-caching

# 6. Create review
ti review new a3f --base main
# → Created review 8b2c (branch: sc-caching, base: main)

# 7. Add reviewers
ti review add-reviewer 8b2c alice@example.com

# 8. Reviewer leaves comments
ti review comment 8b2c "Need to handle cache stampede"
ti review request-changes 8b2c "See comment about stampede"

# 9. Author pushes fixes, updates review
ti review update 8b2c
ti review comment 8b2c "Added stampede protection with singleflight"

# 10. Reviewer approves
ti review approve 8b2c

# 11. Merge and close
ti review merge 8b2c --execute
ti close a3f
```

## Open Questions

1. **Should `ti review merge --execute` actually run `git merge`?** Or should it just record the merge event and let the user handle git? Probably both options (default: record only, `--execute` does the merge).

2. **GitHub/GitLab sync.** If someone creates a review in ticgit but the repo is on GitHub, should `ti review new` optionally create a GitHub PR too? And should `ti import gh` import PR review state? This is a future concern but worth designing the data model to support it.

3. **Multiple reviews per ticket.** A ticket might go through multiple review rounds (branch gets reset, new approach). The model supports this since reviews have their own UUIDs and a ticket has a `reviews` set.

4. **Review without a ticket.** Should reviews be creatable standalone? Probably yes - quick fixes don't always need a ticket first. The `ticket` field would be optional.

5. **Integration with `ti context`.** The agent context command should include active review state when an agent is working on a ticket that has an open review.